- [Artists](/wiki/List_of_chiptune_artists)- [Demoscene](/wiki/Demoscene)|**Chiptune**, also called **8-bit music** (although not all chiptune is 8-bit), is a style of [electronic music](/wiki/Electronic_music), and its associated subculture,[[18]](#cite_note-18)[[19]](#cite_note-19) made using the [programmable sound generator](/wiki/Programmable_sound_generator) (PSG) [sound chips](/wiki/Sound_chip) or [synthesizers](/wiki/Synthesizer) in vintage [arcade machines](/wiki/Arcade_game), [computers](/wiki/Computer) and [video game consoles](/wiki/Video_game_console).[[20]](#cite_note-20) The term is commonly used to refer to [tracker format music](/wiki/Tracker_music) using extremely basic and small [samples](/wiki/Sample_(music)) that an old computer or console could produce (this is the original meaning of the term), as well as music that combines PSG sounds with modern musical styles.[[21]](#cite_note-21)[[22]](#cite_note-22)[[23]](#cite_note-23) It has been described as "an interpretation of many genres". Any existing song can be arranged in a chiptune style. It can be defined by choice of instrument, by [timbre](/wiki/Timbre) more than specific style elements.[[24]](#cite_note-24)## Technology[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=1)]A [waveform generator](/wiki/Waveform_generator) is a fundamental module in a sound synthesis system. A waveform generator usually produces a basic geometrical waveform with a fixed or variable [timbre](/wiki/Timbre) and variable pitch. Common waveform generator configurations usually included two or three simple waveforms and often a single [pseudo-random-noise](/wiki/Pseudo-random-noise) generator (PRNG). Available [waveforms](/wiki/Waveform) often included [pulse wave](/wiki/Pulse_wave) (whose [timbre](/wiki/Timbre) can be varied by modifying the [duty cycle](/wiki/Duty_cycle)), [square wave](/wiki/Square_wave_(waveform)) (a symmetrical pulse wave producing only odd overtones), [triangle wave](/wiki/Triangle_wave) (which has a fixed timbre containing only odd [harmonics](/wiki/Harmonic) but is softer than a square wave), and [sawtooth wave](/wiki/Sawtooth_wave) (which has a bright raspy timbre and contains odd and even harmonics). Two notable examples of systems employing this technology were the [Game Boy](/wiki/Game_Boy) portable game console and the [Commodore 64](/wiki/Commodore_64) personal computer. The Game Boy uses two pulse channels (switchable between 12.5%, 25%, 50% and 75% wave duty cycle), a channel for a 4-bit waveform generator, and a pseudo-random-noise generator. The Commodore 64 however used the [MOS Technology SID](/wiki/MOS_Technology_SID) chip which offered 3 channels, each switchable between pulse, saw-tooth, triangle, and noise. Unlike the Game Boy, the pulse channels on the Commodore 64 allowed full control over wave duty cycles. The SID was a very technically advanced chip, offering many other features including ring modulation and adjustable resonance filters.[[25]](#cite_note-25)Due to the limited number of voices in early sound chips, one of the main challenges is to produce rich [polyphonic](/wiki/Polyphonic) music with them. The usual method to emulate it is via quick [arpeggios](/wiki/Arpeggio), which is one of the most relevant features of chiptune music (along with its electronic timbres).[[26]](#cite_note-26)Some older systems featured a simple [beeper](/wiki/Beep_(sound)) as their only sound output, as the original [ZX Spectrum](/wiki/ZX_Spectrum) and [IBM PC](/wiki/IBM_PC); despite this, many skilled programmers were able to produce unexpectedly rich music with this bare hardware, where the sound is fully generated by the system's [CPU](/wiki/CPU) by direct control of the beeper.## History[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=2)]### 1951–1979: Precursors[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=3)]| External videos |
| --- |
| [![video icon](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg/20px-Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg.png)](/wiki/File:Nuvola_apps_kaboodle.svg) [Science International*: *What Will They Think Of Next?*](https://youtube.com/watch?v=kPzA2jkN0vs&lc=CJKoHtM60L9wUdoAxfRE2dOhjSt8h2QdVNNECbuBPWk), YouTube video |The earliest precursors to chip music can be found in the early history of [computer music](/wiki/Computer_music). In 1951, the computers [CSIRAC](/wiki/CSIRAC) and [Ferranti Mark 1](/wiki/Ferranti_Mark_1) were used to perform real-time synthesized digital music in public.[[27]](#cite_note-27)One of the earliest commercial computer music albums came from the First Philadelphia Computer Music Festival, held August 25, 1978, as part of the Personal Computing '78 show. The First Philadelphia Computer Music Festival recordings were published by Creative Computing in 1979.[[28]](#cite_note-28) The [Global TV](/wiki/Global_TV) program *[Science International](/wiki/Science_International)* (1976–1979) credited a [PDP-11/10](/wiki/PDP-11) for the music.[[29]](#cite_note-29)### Mid-1970s–1980s: Video game origins[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=4)]Chiptune music began to appear with the [video game music](/wiki/Video_game_music) produced during the [golden age of video arcade games](/wiki/Golden_age_of_video_arcade_games). An early example was the opening tune in [Tomohiro Nishikado](/wiki/Tomohiro_Nishikado)'s [arcade game](/wiki/Arcade_game) *[Gun Fight](/wiki/Gun_Fight)* (1975). The first video game to use a continuous background soundtrack was Tomohiro Nishikado's 1978 release *[Space Invaders](/wiki/Space_Invaders)*, which had four simple [chromatic](/wiki/Diatonic_and_chromatic) descending [bass notes](/wiki/Bass_note) repeating in [a loop](/wiki/Music_loop), though it was dynamic and interacted with the player, increasing pace as the enemies descended on the player.[[30]](#cite_note-30)
The first video game to feature continuous melodic [background music](/wiki/Background_music) was *[Rally-X](/wiki/Rally-X)*, an arcade game released by [Namco](/wiki/Namco) in 1980, featuring a simple tune that repeats continuously during [gameplay](/wiki/Gameplay).[[31]](#cite_note-gradar2-31)
It was also one of the earliest games to use a [digital-to-analog converter](/wiki/Digital-to-analog_converter) to produce [sampled](/wiki/Sampling_(signal_processing)) sounds.[[32]](#cite_note-collins_12-32)
That same year, the first video game to feature [speech synthesis](/wiki/Speech_synthesis) was also released, [Sunsoft](/wiki/Sunsoft)'s [shoot 'em up](/wiki/Shoot_%27em_up) arcade game *[Stratovox](/wiki/Stratovox)*.[[31]](#cite_note-gradar2-31)In the late 1970s, the pioneering [synth-pop](/wiki/Synth-pop)/[electronic dance music](/wiki/Electronic_dance_music) group [Yellow Magic Orchestra](/wiki/Yellow_Magic_Orchestra) (YMO) were using computers to produce synthesized music.[[33]](#cite_note-Sarasota-33)
Some of their early music, including their 1978 self-titled [debut album](/wiki/Yellow_Magic_Orchestra_(album)), were [sampling](/wiki/Sampling_(music)) sounds from popular arcade games such as *Space Invaders*[[10]](#cite_note-wire_1996-10) and *Gun Fight*. In addition to incorporating sounds from contemporary video games into their music, the band would later have a major influence on much of the video game and chiptune music produced during the [8-bit](/wiki/History_of_video_game_consoles_(third_generation)) and [16-bit eras](/wiki/History_of_video_game_consoles_(fourth_generation)).[[34]](#cite_note-34)[[35]](#cite_note-35) [Sega](/wiki/Sega)'s 1982 arcade game *[Super Locomotive](/wiki/Super_Locomotive)* for example featured a chiptune [cover version](/wiki/Cover_version) of YMO's "[Rydeen](/wiki/Solid_State_Survivor)" (1979);[[36]](#cite_note-36) several later [computer games](/wiki/PC_game) also covered the song, such as *Trooper Truck* (1983) by [Rabbit Software](/wiki/Rabbit_Software) as well as *[Daley Thompson's Decathlon](/wiki/Daley_Thompson%27s_Decathlon)* (1984) and *[Stryker's Run](/wiki/Stryker%27s_Run)* (1986) arranged by [Martin Galway](/wiki/Martin_Galway).By 1983, [Konami](/wiki/Konami)'s arcade game *[Gyruss](/wiki/Gyruss)* utilized five sound chips along with a digital-to-analog converter, which were partly used to create an electronic rendition of [J. S. Bach's](/wiki/Johann_Sebastian_Bach) *[Toccata and Fugue in D minor](/wiki/Toccata_and_Fugue_in_D_minor,_BWV_565)*.[[37]](#cite_note-37)
In 1984, former YMO member [Haruomi Hosono](/wiki/Haruomi_Hosono) released an album produced entirely from Namco arcade game samples entitled *Video Game Music*, an early example of a chiptune record[[38]](#cite_note-discogs_hosono-38)
and the first video game music album.[[39]](#cite_note-39)
The record featured the work of Namco's chiptune composers: Toshio Kai (*[Pac-Man](/wiki/Pac-Man)* in 1980), Nobuyuki Ohnogi (*[Galaga](/wiki/Galaga)*, *[New Rally-X](/wiki/New_Rally-X)* and *[Bosconian](/wiki/Bosconian)* in 1981, and *[Pole Position](/wiki/Pole_Position_(video_game))* in 1982), and Yuriko Keino (*[Dig Dug](/wiki/Dig_Dug)* and *[Xevious](/wiki/Xevious)* in 1982).[[40]](#cite_note-vgmdb_namco-40)### Early 1980s–1994: FM synthesis[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=5)]A major advance for chip music was the introduction of [frequency modulation synthesis](/wiki/Frequency_modulation_synthesis) (FM synthesis), first commercially released by [Yamaha](/wiki/Yamaha_Corporation) for their [digital synthesizers](/wiki/Digital_synthesizer) and FM [sound chips](/wiki/Sound_chip), which began appearing in arcade machines from the early 1980s.[[41]](#cite_note-karen_10-1-41)[[42]](#cite_note-42)
Arcade game composers utilizing FM synthesis at the time included Konami's [Miki Higashino](/wiki/Miki_Higashino) (*[Gradius](/wiki/Gradius)*, *[Yie-Ar Kung Fu](/wiki/Yie-Ar_Kung_Fu)*, *[Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles](/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles_(arcade_game))*) and [Sega](/wiki/Sega)'s [Hiroshi Kawaguchi](/wiki/Hiroshi_Kawaguchi_(composer)) (*[Space Harrier](/wiki/Space_Harrier)*, *[Hang-On](/wiki/Hang-On)*, *[Out Run](/wiki/Out_Run)*).By the early 1980s, significant improvements to [personal computer game](/wiki/Personal_computer_game) music were made possible with the introduction of [digital](/wiki/Digital_synthesizer) FM synthesis sound. [Yamaha](/wiki/Yamaha_Corporation) began manufacturing FM [synth boards](/wiki/Sound_card) for Japanese computers such as the [NEC PC-8801](/wiki/NEC_PC-8801) and [PC-9801](/wiki/NEC_PC-9801) in the early 1980s, and by the mid-1980s, the PC-8801 and [FM-7](/wiki/FM-7) had built-in FM sound. This allowed computer game music to have greater complexity than the simplistic [beeps](/wiki/Beep_(sound)) from internal speakers. These FM synth boards produced a "warm and pleasant sound" that musicians such as [Yuzo Koshiro](/wiki/Yuzo_Koshiro) and [Takeshi Abo](/wiki/Takeshi_Abo) utilized to produce music that is still highly regarded within the chiptune community.[[43]](#cite_note-hg101_retro-43)
In the early 1980s, Japanese [personal computers](/wiki/Personal_computer) such as the NEC PC-88 and PC-98 featured [audio programming languages](/wiki/Audio_programming_language) such as [Music Macro Language](/wiki/Music_Macro_Language) (MML) and [MIDI](/wiki/MIDI) interfaces, which were most often used to produce video game music.[[44]](#cite_note-shimazu104-44)[Fujitsu](/wiki/Fujitsu) also released the *FM Sound Editor* software for the FM-7 in 1985, providing users with a user-friendly interface to create and edit synthesized music.[[45]](#cite_note-45)In 1987, FM synthesis became available for Western computers when Canadian company [Ad Lib](/wiki/Ad_Lib,_Inc.) released the AdLib Music Synthesizer Card for the [IBM Personal Computer](/wiki/IBM_Personal_Computer),[[46]](#cite_note-46) while Singapore-based [Creative Labs](/wiki/Creative_Labs) incorporated the AdLib card's sound chip into its [Sound Blaster](/wiki/Sound_Blaster) card in 1989.[[47]](#cite_note-47) Both cards were widely supported by [MS-DOS](/wiki/MS-DOS) game developers in the late 1980s and early 1990s.The widespread adoption of FM synthesis by consoles would later be one of the major advances of the [16-bit era](/wiki/History_of_video_game_consoles_(fourth_generation)), by which time 16-bit arcade machines were using multiple FM synthesis chips.[[41]](#cite_note-karen_10-1-41) A major chiptune composer during this period was [Yuzo Koshiro](/wiki/Yuzo_Koshiro).[[48]](#cite_note-santos_2006-48)
Despite later advances in audio technology, he would continue to use older PC-8801 hardware to produce chiptune soundtracks for series such as *[Streets of Rage](/wiki/Streets_of_Rage_(series))* (1991–1994) and *[Etrian Odyssey](/wiki/Etrian_Odyssey)* (2007–present).[[43]](#cite_note-hg101_retro-43) His soundtrack to *[The Revenge of Shinobi](/wiki/The_Revenge_of_Shinobi_(1989_video_game))* (1989) featured [house](/wiki/House_music)[[49]](#cite_note-greening_kotowski-49)[[50]](#cite_note-50)
and [progressive](/wiki/Progressive_electronic_dance_music) [techno](/wiki/Techno) compositions[[48]](#cite_note-santos_2006-48) that fused [electronic dance music](/wiki/Electronic_dance_music) with traditional [Japanese music](/wiki/Music_of_Japan).[[51]](#cite_note-51)
[The soundtrack](/wiki/Discography_of_the_Streets_of_Rage_series) for *[Streets of Rage 2](/wiki/Streets_of_Rage_2)* (1992) is considered "revolutionary" and "ahead of its time" for its "[blend of](/wiki/Electro_house) swaggering house [synths](/wiki/Synthesizer), [dirty](/wiki/Electro_house) [electro-funk](/wiki/Electro_(music)) and [trancey](/wiki/Trance_music) electronic textures that would feel as comfortable in a [nightclub](/wiki/Nightclub) as a video game."[[52]](#cite_note-52)
For the soundtrack to *[Streets of Rage 3](/wiki/Streets_of_Rage_3)* (1994), Koshiro created a new composition method called the "Automated Composing System" to produce "fast-beat techno like [jungle](/wiki/Drum_and_bass)",[[53]](#cite_note-jeff_davis-53)
resulting in innovative and [experimental sounds](/wiki/Experimental_music) generated automatically.[[54]](#cite_note-sega_horowitz-54)
Koshiro also composed chiptune soundtracks for series such as *[Dragon Slayer](/wiki/Dragon_Slayer_(series))*, *[Ys](/wiki/Ys_(video_game))*, *[Shinobi](/wiki/Shinobi_(series))*, and *[ActRaiser](/wiki/ActRaiser)*. Another important FM synth composer was the late [Ryu Umemoto](/wiki/Ryu_Umemoto), who composed chiptune soundtracks for various [visual novel](/wiki/Visual_novel) and [shoot 'em up](/wiki/Shoot_%27em_up) games.[[55]](#cite_note-55)### 1986–present: SID music culture[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=6)]
See also: [MOS Technology SID](/wiki/MOS_Technology_SID) and [Demoscene](/wiki/Demoscene)
[*](/wiki/File:MOS_Technologies_6581.jpg)MOS 6581 and 8580 Commodore 64 SID chipsLater on, several demo groups moved to using their own music instead of ripped game music. In 1986, Jeroen "Red" Kimmel studied [Rob Hubbard](/wiki/Rob_Hubbard)'s player routine and used it for original demo songs[[56]](#cite_note-56)
before writing a routine of his own in 1987. Hobbyists were also writing their own dedicated music editor software, such as [Chris Hülsbeck](/wiki/Chris_H%C3%BClsbeck)'s Soundmonitor* which was released as a type-in listing in a 1986 issue of the German C-64 magazine *[64'er](/w/index.php?title=64%27er&action=edit&redlink=1)*.[[57]](#cite_note-57)The practice of SID music composition has continued seamlessly until this day in conjunction with the [Commodore 64](/wiki/Commodore_64) demoscene. [The High Voltage SID Collection](/wiki/The_High_Voltage_SID_Collection), a comprehensive archive of SID music, contains over 55,000 pieces of SID music.[[58]](#cite_note-58)## Mainstream popularity[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=7)]The heyday of chiptune music was the 1980s.[[59]](#cite_note-bbc_chiptune-59) The earliest commercial chiptune records produced entirely from [sampling](/wiki/Sampling_(music)) [arcade game](/wiki/Arcade_game) sounds have existed since the mid-1980s, an early example being [Haruomi Hosono](/wiki/Haruomi_Hosono)'s *Video Game Music* in 1984.[[38]](#cite_note-discogs_hosono-38) Though entirely chiptune records were uncommon at the time, many mainstream musicians in the [pop rock](/wiki/Pop_rock),[[60]](#cite_note-timemag-60) [hip hop](/wiki/Hip_hop_music)[[61]](#cite_note-61) and [electronic music](/wiki/Electronic_music)[[62]](#cite_note-allmusic_electro-62)
genres were sampling arcade game sounds and [bleeps](/wiki/Beep_(sound)) during the [golden age of video arcade games](/wiki/Golden_age_of_video_arcade_games) (late 1970s to mid-1980s), as early as [Yellow Magic Orchestra](/wiki/Yellow_Magic_Orchestra)'s "[Computer Game](/wiki/Yellow_Magic_Orchestra_(album))" in 1978.[[10]](#cite_note-wire_1996-10) [Buckner & Garcia](/wiki/Buckner_%26_Garcia)'s "[Pac-Man Fever](/wiki/Pac-Man_Fever_(song))" and the [album of the same name](/wiki/Pac-Man_Fever_(album)) were major hits in 1982.[[60]](#cite_note-timemag-60) Arcade game sounds were one of the foundational elements of the [electro music](/wiki/Electro_music) genre, which in turn inspired many other [electronic dance music](/wiki/Electronic_dance_music) genres such as [techno](/wiki/Techno) and [house music](/wiki/House_music), which were sometimes referred to as "[bleep music](/wiki/Bleep_(music))".[[10]](#cite_note-wire_1996-10) *[Space Invaders](/wiki/Space_Invaders)* inspired [Player One's "Space Invaders" (1979)](/wiki/Space_Invaders_(Player_One_song)), which in turn provided the [bassline](/wiki/Bassline) for [Jesse Saunders](/wiki/Jesse_Saunders)' "On and On" (1984),[[63]](#cite_note-63)[[64]](#cite_note-bp-64)
the first [Chicago house](/wiki/Chicago_house) track.[[65]](#cite_note-65) [Warp](/wiki/Warp_(record_label))'s record "[Testone](/w/index.php?title=WarpVision&action=edit&redlink=1)" (1990) by [Sweet Exorcist](/wiki/Sweet_Exorcist_(band)) sampled video game sounds from Yellow Magic Orchestra's "Computer Game" and defined Sheffield's [bleep techno](/wiki/Yorkshire_Bleeps_and_Bass) scene in the early 1990s.[[9]](#cite_note-sicko_brewster_76-9)After the 1980s, however, chiptune music began declining in popularity.[[59]](#cite_note-bbc_chiptune-59) Since then, up until the 2000s, chip music was rarely performed live and the songs were nearly exclusively spread as executable programs and other computer file formats. Some of the earliest examples of record label releases of pure chip music can be found in the late 1990s.[[66]](#cite_note-66)
Chiptune music began gaining popularity again towards the end of the 1990s. The first [electroclash](/wiki/Electroclash) record, [I-F](/wiki/I-F)'s "Space Invaders Are Smoking Grass" (1997), has been described as "burbling electro in a vocodered homage to [Atari-era](/wiki/Golden_age_of_arcade_video_games) hi-jinks".[[67]](#cite_note-Lynskey2002-67)By the mid-2000s, 8-bit chip music began making a comeback in mainstream pop music, when it was used by acts such as [Beck](/wiki/Beck) (for example, the 2005 song "[Girl](/wiki/Girl_(Beck_song))"), [The Killers](/wiki/The_Killers) (for example, the 2004 song "[On Top](/wiki/Hot_Fuss)"), [No Doubt](/wiki/No_Doubt) with the song "[Running](/wiki/Running_(No_Doubt_song))", and particularly [The Postal Service](/wiki/The_Postal_Service) in many of their songs. The low-quality digital [PCM](/wiki/PCM) styling of early game music composers such as Hiroshi Kawaguchi also began gaining popularity.[[68]](#cite_note-68)
In 2003, the [J-pop](/wiki/J-pop) girl group [Perfume](/wiki/Perfume_(Japanese_band)),[[69]](#cite_note-japantimes-69)[[70]](#cite_note-bounce_perfume-70)
along with producer [Yasutaka Nakata](/wiki/Yasutaka_Nakata), began producing music combining chiptunes with [synth-pop](/wiki/Synth-pop) and [electro house](/wiki/Electro_house);[[70]](#cite_note-bounce_perfume-70) their breakthrough came in 2007 with *[Game](/wiki/Game_(Perfume_album))*, which led to other Japanese female artists using a similar electronic style, including [Aira Mitsuki](/wiki/Aira_Mitsuki), [immi](/wiki/Immi), [Mizca](/wiki/Masami_Mitsuoka), [SAWA](/wiki/Sawa_(singer)), [Saori@destiny](/wiki/Saori_at_Destiny), and [Sweet Vacation](/wiki/Sweet_Vacation).[[71]](#cite_note-allabout-71)
Electro house producer [Deadmau5](/wiki/Deadmau5) started his career in the late 1990s, with a chiptune and demoscene movements-influenced sound. Three self-released compilations, [Project 56](/wiki/Project_56_(album)), [deadmau5 Circa 1998–2002](/wiki/Deadmau5_Circa_1998%E2%80%932002) and [A Little Oblique](/wiki/A_Little_Oblique), were finished in 2006.[[72]](#cite_note-72)In 2007, the entirely chiptune album *[8-Bit Operators: The Music of Kraftwerk](/wiki/8-Bit_Operators:_The_Music_of_Kraftwerk)* was released on major mainstream label [Astralwerks](/wiki/Astralwerks)/[EMI](/wiki/EMI) Records, which included several prominent and noted chipmusicians, including Nanoloop[[73]](#cite_note-73)
creator Oliver Wittchow, and LittleSoundDJ[[74]](#cite_note-74)
creator Johan Kotlinski who appears as the artist *Role Model*. [Kraftwerk](/wiki/Kraftwerk) founding member [Ralf Hütter](/wiki/Ralf_H%C3%BCtter) personally selected the tracks.[[75]](#cite_note-75)
A vinyl 12-inch single version was released on February 24, 2007 as a precursor to the full-length CD, and reached as high as number 17[[76]](#cite_note-76)
on the *Billboard* magazine Hot Dance Singles Sales Chart. In March 2007, the CD release reached as high as number 1 on the [CMJ](/wiki/CMJ) RPM (North American college Electronic) charts.[[77]](#cite_note-77)[[78]](#cite_note-78)
Edinburgh-born electronic musician [Unicorn Kid](/wiki/Unicorn_Kid) has helped further popularize chiptune, especially with the song "True Love Fantasy" and other songs from the EP "Tidal Rave" being played on late night radio, including on [BBC Radio 1](/wiki/BBC_Radio_1), where he played live on the Festive Festival 2011. In Canada, [Eightcubed](/wiki/Eightcubed) and [Crystal Castles](/wiki/Crystal_Castles_(band)) helped the popularity further via the Toronto underground club scene and created a lasting impression with the music video "Heart Invaders" debuting on [MuchMusic](/wiki/MuchMusic) in 2008[[79]](#cite_note-79)
and the single "[Alice Practice](/wiki/Alice_Practice)" hitting 29th on [NME](/wiki/NME) "150 Best Tracks of the Past 15 Years".[[80]](#cite_note-80)During the late 2000s, a new wave of chiptune culture took place, boosted by the release of software such as LittleSoundDJ for the [Game Boy](/wiki/Game_Boy_family). This new culture has much more emphasis on live performances and record releases than the demoscene and tracker culture, of which the new artists are often only distantly aware.[[81]](#cite_note-81)
In recent years, 8-bit chiptune sounds, or "video game beats", have been used by a number of mainstream pop artists. Examples include artists such as [Kesha](/wiki/Kesha)[[82]](#cite_note-82)
(most notably in "[Tik Tok](/wiki/Tik_Tok_(song))",[[83]](#cite_note-Puls-83) the [best-selling single](/wiki/List_of_best-selling_singles) of 2010[[84]](#cite_note-ifpi10-84)),
[50 Cent](/wiki/50_Cent) with the hit single "Ayo Technology", [Robyn](/wiki/Robyn), [Snoop Dogg](/wiki/Snoop_Dogg),[[83]](#cite_note-Puls-83)
[Eminem](/wiki/Eminem) (for example, "Hellbound"), [Nelly Furtado](/wiki/Nelly_Furtado), and [Timbaland](/wiki/Timbaland) (see [Timbaland plagiarism controversy](/wiki/Timbaland_plagiarism_controversy)). The influence of video game sounds can also be heard in contemporary British [electronica](/wiki/Electronica) music by artists such as [Dizzee Rascal](/wiki/Dizzee_Rascal) and [Kieran Hebden](/wiki/Kieran_Hebden),[[85]](#cite_note-guardian_ymo-85)
as well as in [heavy metal](/wiki/Heavy_metal_music) bands such as [DragonForce](/wiki/DragonForce). [Grime](/wiki/Grime_music) music in particular samples sawtooth wave sounds from video games which were popular in [East London](/wiki/East_(London_sub_region)).[[11]](#cite_note-Jong-11)
Some [dubstep](/wiki/Dubstep) producers have also been influenced by video game chiptunes, particularly the work of [Yuzo Koshiro](/wiki/Yuzo_Koshiro).[[86]](#cite_note-86)[[87]](#cite_note-self-titledmag-87)[[88]](#cite_note-timeout_20425-88)
In 2010, a [BBC News](/wiki/BBC_News) article stated that the "sights and sounds of [old-school games](/wiki/Retrogaming)" (naming *[Frogger](/wiki/Frogger)* and *[Donkey Kong](/wiki/Donkey_Kong)* as examples) are "now becoming a part of mainstream music and culture."[[59]](#cite_note-bbc_chiptune-59)
[Complextro](/wiki/Complextro) pioneer [Porter Robinson](/wiki/Porter_Robinson) has also cited video game sounds, or chiptunes, as an influence on his style of music along with 1980s [analog synth](/wiki/Analog_synthesizer) music.[[89]](#cite_note-nashville_complextro-89) In 2022, [trap music](/wiki/Trap_music) producer Popstar Benny cited video game sounds as one of the foundations for the [plugg music](/wiki/Plugg_music) genre.[[13]](#cite_note-SC-13)## Tracker chiptunes[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=8)]
See also: [music tracker](/wiki/Music_tracker) and [module file](/wiki/Module_file)The [Commodore Amiga](/wiki/Amiga) (1985) with its sample-based sound generation distanced the concept of microcomputer music away from plain chip-synthesized sounds. Amiga [tracker music software](/wiki/Music_tracker), beginning from Karsten Obarski's [Ultimate Soundtracker](/wiki/Ultimate_Soundtracker) (1987), inspired great numbers of computer enthusiasts to create computer music. As an offshoot of the burgeoning tracker music culture, a type of tracker music reminiscent of Commodore 64 SID music was born, that utilized simple waveforms instead of digitized samples. This type of music with small samples came to be called "chiptunes" referring to Amiga's chip memory where such samples would easily fit.Earliest examples of tracker chiptunes date back to 1989 and are attributed to the [demoscene](/wiki/Demoscene) musicians [4mat](/wiki/4mat), Baroque, [TDK](/wiki/Mark_Knight_(sound_designer)), Turtle and Duz. Tracker chiptunes are based on very short looped waveforms which are modulated by tracker effects such as [arpeggio](/wiki/Arpeggio), [vibrato](/wiki/Vibrato), and [portamento](/wiki/Portamento). A very common loop length is 128 samples, which at an approximate sample rate of 17 kHz misses a C note by a few [cents](/wiki/Cent_(music)).There is at least one commercial game for the Amiga, Nebulus II, that used chiptune style music, although with some conventional sampled instrument sounds as well as speech. The game apparently was initially planned for release for the C64 but was canceled.The small amount of sample data made tracker chiptunes far more space-efficient than most other types of tracker music, which made them appealing to size-limited demoscene demos and [crack intros](/wiki/Crack_intro). Tracker chiptunes have also been commonly used in other [warez scene](/wiki/Warez_scene) executables such as [keygens](/wiki/Keygen).Nowadays the term "chiptune" is also used to cover chip music using actual chip-based synthesis, but some sources such as the Amiga Music Preservation project still define a chiptune specifically as a small [tracker module](/wiki/Module_file).[[90]](#cite_note-90) Modern trackers used today include OpenMPT, Famitracker, Furnace and Goattracker.## Contemporary chiptune music[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=9)]
