Problems playing these files? See [media help](/wiki/Help:Media).*The chip [scene](/wiki/Warez_scene) has become relevant thanks to "[compos](/wiki/Compo_(demoscene))" being held, groups releasing [music disks](/wiki/Music_disk) and with the [cracktro](/wiki/Crack_intro)/demo scene. New [tracker](/wiki/Tracker_(music_software)) tools are used for making chip sounds available to less tech-savvy musicians. The [NES](/wiki/Nintendo_Entertainment_System) platform has the MidiNES, a cartridge that turns the system into a full blown hardware MIDI controlled synthesizer. Around 2007, the Mssiah was released for the [Commodore 64](/wiki/Commodore_64), which is very similar to the MidiNES, but with greater parameter controls, sequencing, analog drum emulation, and limited sample playback. The [Commodore PET](/wiki/Commodore_PET) has the [open-source](/wiki/Open-source_software) [PetSynth](/wiki/PetSynth) software, which uses the PET's [6522](/wiki/6522) chip for sound, allows the computer to be played like a piano keyboard, and features many effects. For MS-DOS, [Fast Tracker](/wiki/FastTracker_2) is one of the most famous chiptune makers because of the ability to create hand-drawn samples with the mouse. Chiptune artist [Pixelh8](/wiki/Pixelh8) has also designed music software such as Music Tech[[91]](#cite_note-91)
for the Game Boy and the Pro Performer[[92]](#cite_note-92)
for the [Game Boy Advance](/wiki/Game_Boy_Advance) and [Nintendo DS](/wiki/Nintendo_DS) which turn both machines into real time synthesizers.There have been a number of television segments featuring chiptunes and chip music artists in the past few years. On April 11, 2005, [8 Bit Weapon](/wiki/8_Bit_Weapon) played their songs "Bombs Away" and "Gameboy Rocker" on [G4](/wiki/G4_(U.S._TV_channel))'s *[Attack of the Show](/wiki/Attack_of_the_Show)* live broadcast Episode #5058.[[93]](#cite_note-93)[[94]](#cite_note-94) In 2008, as a parody of *[Masterpiece Theatre](/wiki/Masterpiece_(TV_series))*, the first four episodes of *[Boing Boing Video](/wiki/Boing_Boing#Boing_Boing_Video)*'s *[SPAMasterpiece Theater](/wiki/SPAMasterpiece_Theater)* opened with a chiptune remix of [Jean-Joseph Mouret](/wiki/Jean-Joseph_Mouret)'s "[Rondeau: Fanfare](/wiki/Fanfare-Rondeau)" (1735) by Hamhocks Buttermilk Johnson.[[95]](#cite_note-Boing_Boing_tv_2008a-95)
Another chipmusic feature included little-scale, Dot.AY, [Ten Thousand Free Men & Their Families](/wiki/Ten_Thousand_Free_Men_%26_Their_Families) and Jim Cuomo on the Australian television series *[Good Game](/wiki/Good_Game_(TV_program))* in 2009.[[96]](#cite_note-96)The [Electronic Frontier Foundation](/wiki/Electronic_Frontier_Foundation) in December 2010 used a faux 8-bit game with an 8-bit sound track by crashfaster to demonstrate its notable legal achievements for that year.[[97]](#cite_note-97)In March 2012, the [Smithsonian American Art Museum](/wiki/Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum)'s "The Art of Video Games" exhibit opened featuring a chipmusic soundtrack at the entrance by artists [8 Bit Weapon](/wiki/8_Bit_Weapon) and [ComputeHer](/wiki/ComputeHer). 8 Bit Weapon also created a track called "The art of Video Games Anthem" for the exhibit.[[98]](#cite_note-98) In September 2015, the first music compilation based on [Domo (NHK)](/wiki/Domo_(NHK)), Domo Loves Chiptune, was released on iTunes, Amazon, and all major music streaming services.[[99]](#cite_note-Domo_Loves_Chiptune_Promotional_Video-99)[[100]](#cite_note-Everybody_Loves_Chiptune_&_So_Do_We-100)
The compilation features top artists in the Chiptune genre such as [Anamanaguchi](/wiki/Anamanaguchi) and [Disasterpeace](/wiki/Disasterpeace). Domo Loves Chiptune also features the first Chiptune remix of the Domo theme song by Mystery Mansion. The New York City chiptune scene was also the subject of a documentary called *Reformat the Planet* by [2 Player Productions](/wiki/2_Player_Productions). This film was an official selection at the 2008 [South by Southwest](/wiki/South_by_Southwest).[[101]](#cite_note-101)Chip music has returned to 21st-century gaming, either in full-chip music style or using chip samples in the music. Popular games that feature chiptune elements in their soundtracks include *[Shovel Knight](/wiki/Shovel_Knight)*[[102]](#cite_note-102) and *[Undertale](/wiki/Undertale)*.[[103]](#cite_note-103)## Events[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=10)]Events take place all around the world that focus around the celebration and recognition of chiptune music.### Blip Festival[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=11)]
Main article: [Blip Festival](/wiki/Blip_Festival)### MAGFest's Chipspace[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Chiptune&action=edit&section=12)]
Main article: [MAGFest](/wiki/MAGFest)In the United States, during [Super MAGFest](/wiki/MAGFest)—a yearly convention that hosts a variety of video game-related events—popular chiptune artists such as [goto80](/wiki/Goto80) and [Chipzel](/wiki/Chipzel) have previously performed on the Concert Hall mainstage. A chiptune-focused mainstage show (aptly named "Chip Rave") typically occurs on the third day of the convention within the concert hall and has featured countless prominent faces in the chiptune community.Super MAGFest also holds a continuous venue called Chipspace, a place where participants in the chiptune community go on-stage and perform their music through an open mic system.[[104]](#cite_note-104) Originally started by Chiptunes=WIN[[105]](#cite_note-105) founder Brandon L. Hood and maintained by geekbeatradio,[[106]](#cite_note-106)
Chipspace has evolved over the course of MAGFest's lifespan to bring chiptune fans closer together.[[107]](#cite_note-107)
